<!DOCTYPE html>
@* The `lang` of the document is the route's to decide, not this file's. A layout declares
   what it needs with the same `props<T>()` a component and a route use, and the route
   resolves it in `@server layout(ctx, data)` — after `load`, and with what `load` brought.

   With a DEFAULT, which is the whole difference a default makes: the fifteen pages of this
   example that are in Spanish say nothing at all, and only the one page whose language comes
   out of its data bothers to answer. A prop with no default would make every route restate
   the obvious. What a REQUIRED one looks like is in `_layout-articulo.fud`, where it belongs:
   there the value is not obvious, and forgetting it is an error worth being told about. *@
<html lang="@culture">
  <head>
    @code {
      const { culture = "es" } = props<{ culture?: string }>();
    }

    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    @* Un fichero PÚBLICO: la URL la elige quien lo escribe, no el build. Va sin hash,
       porque un favicon se pide por su nombre desde sitios que no son este código. La
       ruta se resuelve contra `public/`, así que el fichero se comprueba —si no está es
       `FUD0363`— y el Service Worker lo precachea solo, sin escribir nada en `sw.json`.
       La otra forma es `./algo.svg`, y entonces el nombre lo pone el build. *@
    <link rel="icon" href="/logo.svg">

    @* La misma hoja de tokens que el otro layout, pedida con `?inline`: en vez de una URL
       que el navegador va a buscar, sus reglas viajan DENTRO de la página. Una petición
       menos y nada que esperar; a cambio, esos bytes no se comparten entre páginas ni los
       guarda la caché del navegador. El `?inline` no sustituye nada: lo que el autor
       escribió sigue saliendo, solo que lo lleva el documento. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="../styles/tokens.css?inline">

    <!-- The same marker as the other layout, with the other answer: the coordinator travels
         inside the document instead of being fetched. There are no preloads then, and none
         are needed — its `import`s are read with the HTML, which is the earliest instant
         there is. It is the developer's decision and not the framework's: a strict security
         policy and a slow connection ask for opposite things, and only the person deploying
         knows which of the two they have. -->
    <script type="module" src="fudic:runtime?inline"></script>

    @* Everything each route contributes to the head — its <title>, its component
       stylesheets, the style-adoption polyfill — goes exactly here. *@
    @RenderHead()
  </head>
  <body>
    @* The nav is route-specific (it highlights the current section), so the route
       fills this hole with `@section nav { … }`. A route that declares no `nav`
       renders nothing here, without an error. *@
    @RenderSection(nav)

    <main>@RenderBody()</main>

    <footer class="site">
      <p>fudic — ejemplo básico. El shell de esta página lo escribe un solo fichero.</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
